    "brief": "Parametric workflow with 15 adjustable parameters",
    "principle": "\n\nRelated insight: Workflow scaling: While this pattern uses 15 parameters, more complex variants like '11-3' can involve 28+ components for advanced trigonometric parametric sequences.\n\nRelated insight: Complexity spectrum: From 15 parameters (this pattern) to 28+ components (11-3) to 59 components (S11E04), showing the full range of parametric workflow scaling.\n\nRelated insight: Refined complexity spectrum: 15 parameters (this pattern) → 19 components (S11E05) → 28+ components (11-3) → 59 components (S11E04), showing granular progression in parametric workflow complexity.\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: Extended complexity spectrum: From 15 parameters (this pattern) → 19 components (S11E05) → 28+ components (11-3) → 39 components (14-5) → 59 components (S11E04) → 73 components (14-6), showing the full range from simple parametric sequences to complex surface creation workflows.\n\nRelated insight: Ultimate complexity spectrum: From 15 parameters (this pattern) → 19 components (S11E05) → 28+ components (11-3) → 39 components (14-5) → 59 components (S11E04) → 73 components (14-6) → 154 components (14-7), showing the complete range from simple parametric sequences to highly complex surface workflows.\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: Geometric specialization branches: While this pattern focuses on trigonometric operations, the 28-component '4-qos' pattern demonstrates Islamic geometry specialization, showing how parametric workflows can branch into domain-specific geometric applications.\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: S07E01 (82 components) extends the complexity spectrum beyond the previously documented 73-component level, showing that parametric workflows can reach even higher component counts while maintaining the recipe/workflow pattern.",
